			<div class="content" id="post-content-4860093">
									

<p>Responding to PM request: Sorry if I'm late!<br />
That's an interesting SCP you got there<br />
Would be a shame if someone<br />
Critiqued it&#8230;</p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">+&nbsp;show&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">–&nbsp;hide&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<p>I hate to be a sore thumb, but unless you make a some major changes, I don't see this as being a successful idea. Your base concept is okay, but its severely lacking in characters, themes, and the general appeal that stories have. What you have right now, at least to me, seems like a glorified mash of a museum and haunted house, which aren't the most original combination. Granted, I'm not exactly into history, which may be why I find the idea so lackluster.</p>
<p>What is SCP trying to prove? That colonialism/slavery/war/etc were terrible and tragic? Most people know that. I feel like you need to take this a step beyond what you currently have. Beyond simple ghosts and demons and classic, cheap horror. You need something <em>real</em> for your audience.</p>
<p>Here's an alternate idea that you can choose to reject/accept:<br />
Each floor of the room causes the participant to physically enter a pocket dimension, where they reenact a scene from the particular era of the floor. For instance, for the 1500s to 1600s, have the person that enters the hall be chased by bloodhounds and and a white man. Maybe have their family running with them, and maybe their son falls behind. The person turns back and attempts to find the son, but sees the dogs have already torn into him. The person turns back and starts running again, and they can hear bullets hitting tree trunks beside their heads, and the thunder of hoofbeats behind them. They try to keep running forward, barefoot and near naked, but they feel a pain in their back, and see blood seeping out of their chest, where they'd been hit by a bullet. The person then falls over, and feels a boot on his head. In front of him, he can see his family still being chased, and he knows they won't make it. He feels a rifle on his head, and with a bang, everything goes black.</p>
<p>When the person wakes up again, they'll find themselves in an empty room with a door. They'll open the door and descend down a flight of stairs, after which they relive another terrifying and tragic scenario. And for added elements of horror, have it so that there's no way to leave. The person can only go forward. They'll be trapped in there, experiencing history forever and ever. And there's not much they can do about it.</p>
<p>Of course you don't need to take that idea, but you need to evoke some sort of emotional response in your audience, especially if you want to make an emotional impact, which is something I feel you want to do.</p>
<p>I'm not going to give this idea a greenlight, at least not yet. But if you go back and refine it, an give some more details, I'll come back and give it a second look. Please PM me with any questions/comments/concerns, and best of luck!</p>
</div>
</div>
